WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.960
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:12.400
Today we are going to have a look at beautiful&nbsp;
simulations from a quick paper. But wait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:18.560
how can a research paper be quick?&nbsp;
Well, it is quick for two key reasons.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:26.800
Reason number one. Look at this complex soft body&nbsp;
simulation. This is not a jumpsuit, this showcases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:34.320
the geometry of the outer tissue of this elephant,&nbsp;
and is made of 80 thousand elements. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:41.360
hold on to your papers, away with the geometry…and&nbsp;
feast your eyes upon this beautiful simulation!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.640
My goodness, tons of stretching,&nbsp;
moving and deformation. Wow!

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:56.800
So, how long do we have to wait for a&nbsp;
result like this? All-nighters, right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:04.560
about that quick part I just mentioned…it runs&nbsp;
very, very quickly. 8 milliseconds per frame.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.640
Yes, that means that it runs easily in&nbsp;
real time on a modern graphics card.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:17.920
And this work has some other aspect that is&nbsp;
also quick, which we will discuss in a moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.560
But first, let’s see some of the additional&nbsp;
advantages it has compared to previous methods.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.560
For instance, if you think this was a stretchy&nbsp;
simulation, no-no! This is a stretchy simulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:39.840
Look, this is a dragon. Well, it doesn’t&nbsp;
look like a dragon, does it. Why is that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.200
Well, it has been compressed&nbsp;
and scrambled into a tiny plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:53.840
but if we let go of the forces. Ah, there it&nbsp;
is. It was able to regain its original shape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:00.960
And the algorithm can withstand even this sort&nbsp;
of torture test, which is absolutely amazing.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.520
One more key advantage is the lack of&nbsp;
volume dissipation. Yes. Believe it or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.960
many previous simulation methods struggle&nbsp;
with things disappearing over time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:17.200
Don’t believe it? Let me show you this experiment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:25.280
with gooey dragons and bowls. When using a&nbsp;
traditional technique, whoa, this guy is GONE.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.760
So, let’s see what a previous method would do&nbsp;
in this case. We start out with this block,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:39.920
and after a fair bit of stretching..wait a&nbsp;
second. Are you trying to tell me that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:48.560
has the same amount of volume as this? No sir.&nbsp;
This is volume dissipation at its finest. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:58.640
can the new method be so quick, and still retain&nbsp;
the entirety of the volume? Yes sir! Loving it.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.320
Let’s see another example&nbsp;
of volume preservation…okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:10.800
I am loving this…these transformations are not&nbsp;
reasonable, this is indeed a very challenging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:18.960
test. Can it withstand all this? Keep your eyes&nbsp;
on the volume of the cubes, which change a little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:27.040
it’s not perfect, but considering the crazy things&nbsp;
we are doing to it, this is very respectable. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:33.760
in the end, when we let go, we get most of it&nbsp;
back. I’ll freeze the appropriate frame for you.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:42.160
So, second quick aspect. What is it? Yes,&nbsp;
it is quick to run, but that’s not all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:48.800
It is also quick to implement. For reference, if&nbsp;
you wish to implement one of our earlier papers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.960
on material synthesis, this is the&nbsp;
number of variables you have to remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:03.040
and this is the pseudocode for the algorithm&nbsp;
itself. What is that? Well, this shows what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.960
steps we need to take to implement our technique&nbsp;
in a computer program. I don’t consider this to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.160
be too complex, but now, compare it to&nbsp;
this simulation algorithm’s pseudocode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.160
Whoa, much simpler. I would wager&nbsp;
that if everything goes well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.160
a competent computer graphics research&nbsp;
scientist could implement this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:31.200
in a day. And that is a rare sight for a modern&nbsp;
simulation algorithm, and that is excellent.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.160
I think you are going to hear from this&nbsp;
technique a great deal more. Who wrote it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.480
Of course, Miles Macklin and Matthias Müller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:47.520
two excellent research scientists at NVIDIA.&nbsp;
Congratulations, and with this kind of progress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:54.080
just imagine what we will be able to do two more&nbsp;
papers down the line! What a time to be alive!

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.680
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

